ssification."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"7E5ljg8xR9s4"},"source":["# STEP 8 — Preprocessing Pipeline\n","Identify Numerical and Categorical Columns"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"IQ6LJOwOR9s4","executionInfo":{"status":"aborted","timestamp":1787530634257,"user_tz":-600,"elapsed":121937,"user":{"displayName":"Wisnu Wibowo","userId":"15217971142000187842"}}},"outputs":[],"source":["num_features = X.select_dtypes(include=['int64','float64']).columns\n","cat_features = X.select_dtypes(include=['object']).columns\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"G96gBnfb9_Vf"},"source":["This code separates the input features into numerical and categorical feature groups based on their data types. Numerical features (num_features) include integer and float columns, while categorical features (cat_features) include text-based (object) columns. This separation is important for applying different preprocessing techniques such as scaling for numerical data and encoding for categorical data before machine learning modeling."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"kQC8KwQQR9s5"},"source":["Create Transformers"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"zAlGUeEyR9s5","executionInfo":{"status":"aborted","timestamp":1787530634258,"user_tz":-600,"elapsed":121938,"user":{"displayName":"Wisnu Wibowo","userId":"15217971142000187842"}}},"outputs":[],"source":["numeric_transformer = Pipeline(steps=[\n","    ('imputer', SimpleImputer(strategy='median')),\n","    ('scaler', StandardScaler())\n","])\n","\n","categorical_transformer = Pipeline(steps=[\n","    ('imputer', SimpleImputer(strategy='most_frequent')),\n","    ('onehot', OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown='ignore'))\n","])\n","\n","preprocessor = ColumnTransformer(\n","    transformers=[\n","        ('num', numeric_transformer, num_features),\n","        ('cat', categorical_transformer, cat_features)\n","    ])\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"6w17hk9p-HXc"},"source":["This code builds preprocessing pipelines for numerical and categorical features before machine learning modeling. The numerical pipeline fills missing values using the median and standardizes the data using StandardScaler to improve model performance. The categorical pipeline fills missing values with the most frequent category and converts categorical variables into numerical format using OneHotEncoder. Finally, ColumnTransformer combines both preprocessing pipelines so that each feature type is processed automatically and efficiently within a unified workflow."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"f0T5j7zQR9s5"},"source":["# STEP 9 — Train-Test Split"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{"id":"TDo4ruSPR9s5","executionInfo":{"status":"aborted","timestamp":1787530634261,"user_tz":-600,"elapsed":121941,"user":{"displayName":"Wisnu Wibowo","userId":"15217971142000187842"}}},"outputs":[],"source":["X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n","    X, y, test_size=0.2, random_state=1034)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"9mqL58xp-sX8"},"source":["This code splits the dataset into training and testing data for machine learning modeling. The variable X contains the input features, while y contains the target variable (Survived). The parameter test_size=0.2 means that 80% of the data is used for training and 20% is reserved for testing. The random_state=42 parameter ensures that the data split remains consistent and reproducible every time the code is executed.\n","\n","The function of this process is to train the model using the training data (X_train, y_train) and evaluate its performance using unseen testing data (X_test, y_test). This helps measure how well the model generalizes to new data and prevents overfitting.\n","\n","The results of this code are four separate datasets:\n","\n","\n","\n","* X_train → training input features\n","* X_test → testing input features\n","* y_train → training target 