ion Loss'\n",")\n","plt.plot(\n","    list(n_estimators_range),\n","    rf_val_losses,\n","    label='Random Forest Validation Loss'\n",")\n","plt.plot(\n","    range(1, len(xgb_val_losses)+1),\n","    xgb_val_losses,\n","    label='XGBoost Validation Loss'\n",")\n","plt.plot(\n","    range(1, nn_epochs+1),\n","    nn_val_losses,\n","    label='Neural Network Validation Loss'\n",")\n","plt.title('Overall Log Loss Curve Comparison')\n","plt.xlabel('Training Steps/Number of Trees/Boosting Iterations')\n","plt.ylabel('Log Loss')\n","plt.legend()\n","plt.grid(True)\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"2a5c0074"},"source":["### Log Loss Curve Comparison: Key Results\n","\n","The log loss metric is crucial for evaluating binary classification models, quantifying prediction accuracy by penalizing false classifications based on probability output. A lower log loss indicates a more accurate and well-calibrated model, meaning its predicted probabilities are closer to the true labels.\n","\n","**Observed Log Loss Values (from Model Performance Evaluation Matrix):**\n","\n","*   **Logistic Regression:** Achieved a log loss of **0.4066**. This model shows a relatively stable and steadily decreasing log loss curve, indicating consistent learning and good calibration capabilities.\n","*   **Random Forest:** Achieved a log loss of **0.4065**. The log loss generally decreased as the number of estimators increased, eventually plateauing, demonstrating strong predictive power.\n","*   **XGBoost:** Recorded a log loss of **0.4548**. While XGBoost typically achieves low error quickly, in this comparison, its final log loss was slightly higher.\n","*   **Neural Network:** Showed a log loss of **0.4358**. The Neural Network's curve typically exhibited an initial decrease, sometimes with more fluctuations, as its weights were adjusted during training."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{"id":"tlSy_ExiR9s7"},"source":["# STEP 11 — Model Evaluation Function"]},{"cel